# Let the panel toggle the screen blanker, set CPU/GPU frequency policy and
# switch direct-power reporting on or off without running as root.
#
# Every rule is keyed on uid 10000 (see nura-webui.service) because the account
# name differs between installs.
#
# sudo matches the whole command line, so anything else still needs a password:
# `systemctl restart sshd`, `enable evil.service`, extra flags, all fail.
#
# The frequency helper takes an argument, so its sudoers entry is a wildcard on
# the value. That is safe because the script only accepts the three fixed mode
# names and re-validates them itself.
#
# The direct-power unit is oneshot with RemainAfterExit, so `enable` alone
# would leave the bind mounts unmade until the next boot; the panel starts it
# through the same fixed command line. Both verbs are granted explicitly rather
# than as a wildcard so no other unit can be reached through them.
#10000 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable blank_screen.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable --now blank_screen.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable --now direct_power.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl disable --now direct_power.service, \
    /usr/libexec/nura-cpufreq mode *
